c{12}{2} \\\\ x & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-6,\\,y=-8.$"] [17592186562497 "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -1 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186562508 "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $6.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2a+3b=-2 \\\\ \\frac{a}{3}-\\frac{b}{6}=-\\frac{5}{3} & |\\,\\times 6\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2a+3b=-2 \\\\ 2a-b=-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Let's solve for $b$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a-b & =-10 \\\\ b & =2a+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $b=2a+10$ into the first equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+3b & =-2 \\\\ 2a+3(2a+10) & =-2 \\\\ 2a+6a+30 & =-2 \\\\ 8a & =-32 \\\\ a & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $b,$ we substitute $a=-4$ into the equation $b=2a+10$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =2a+10 \\\\  & =2(-4)+10 \\\\  & =-8+10 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-4,\\,b=2.$"] [17592186562510 "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|cc}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{array}\\right],\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-7-\\lambda  & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -2-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )((-7-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-5(-4)) & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +41) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{-10\\pm \\sqrt{(10)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 41}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186562516 "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|cc}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{array}\\right],\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the second and third columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186563678 "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -9 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 10 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 10 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-6\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-6.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-6\\,⇒\\,k=6.\n$$"] [17592186563694 "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply both equations by $100,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0.05a-0.2b=0.04 & |\\times 100 \\\\ 0.12a-1.2b=0.24 & |\\times 100\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5a-20b=4 \\\\ 12a-120b=24\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$ and divide the second equation by $3,$ we will get a $40b$ term in the first equation that can cancel with the $-40b$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5a-20b=4 & |\\,\\times (-2) \\\\ 12a-120b=24 & |\\,÷3\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-10a+40b=-8 \\\\ 4a-40b=8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $b$-variable and allows us to solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}-10a+\\cancel{40b} & =-8 \\\\ 4a-\\cancel{40b} & =8 \\\\ \\hline (-10a+4a) & =(-8+8) \\\\ -6a & =0 \\\\ a & =0\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $a=0$ into the equation $5a-20b=4$ to solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a-20b & =4 \\\\ 5(0)-20b & =4 \\\\ -20b & =4 \\\\ b & =-\\frac{4}{20} \\\\ b & =-0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=0,\\,b=-0.2.$ So,\n\n$$\na+b=0+(-0.2)=-0.2.\n$$"] [17592186563719 "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -7\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -7\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+(-1){a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot (-1)=-2.$"] [17592186563726 "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the top equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3.6v+21.6=0 & |\\,\\times 10 \\\\ 3v+4w+2=0 & \\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}36v+216=0 \\\\ 3v+4w+2=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can easily solve for $v$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36v+216 & =0 \\\\ 36v & =-216 \\\\ v & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the value of $w,$ we can substitute $v=-6$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3v+4w+2 & =0 \\\\ 3(-6)+4w+2 & =0 \\\\ -18+4w+2 & =0 \\\\ 4w & =16 \\\\ w & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $v=-6,\\,w=4.$"] [17592186563732 "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=3\\cdot 2=6.$"] [17592186563749 "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=3 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=18 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3}=9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -8 & 18 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 & 9\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 & 9\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}=6 \\\\ {x}_{3}=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=3.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(3)=3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=9.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{a}_{1}+6{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ 9\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+6\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=9\\cdot 6\\cdot 3=162.$"] [17592186564467 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y-{x}^{2}\\ge 0 \\\\ 2x-y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge {x}^{2} \\\\ y\\ne 2x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge {x}^{2},\\,y\\ne 2x\\}.\n$$"] [17592186564480 "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ 2t-3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{0} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2(0)}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{0}^{2}-0+1 \\\\ 2(0)-3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+1 \\\\ {c}_{2}-3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1=2 \\\\ {c}_{2}-3=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=1.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ 2t-3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186564483 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-6y-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-6y-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4-2x & -6-6y\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4-2x=0 \\\\ -6-6y=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n4-2x=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n-6-6y=0\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(2,-1)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-2x)=-2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-6-6y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-2x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-6-6y)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(2,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(2,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,-1)=det({f}^{″}(2,-1))=(-2)\\cdot (-6)-{0}^{2}=12>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)=({f}^{″}(2,-1))_{11}=-2<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(2,-1).$"] [17592186564487 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+20{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-13{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 20 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -13 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -13 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(0)+9(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true."] [17592186564495 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\n \nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ is an stationary point of $f(x,y).$  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=det({f}^{″}(0,1))={0}^{2}-1\\cdot 1-=-1<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,1)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,1).$"] [17592186564498 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y>0 \\\\ {x}^{3}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y>0 \\\\ x\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>0,\\,x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$"] [17592186564501 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-6{t}^{2}+\\frac{41}{3}t-8 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}t+5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-6{t}^{2}+\\frac{41}{3}t-8 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}t+5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(\\frac{5{a}_{1}-8{a}_{2}}{3}\\right){t}^{2}+\\left(\\frac{5{b}_{1}-8{b}_{2}}{3}\\right)t+\\left(\\frac{5{c}_{1}-8{c}_{2}}{3}\\right) \\\\ \\left(\\frac{-4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}}{3}\\right){t}^{2}+\\left(\\frac{-4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}}{3}\\right)t+\\left(\\frac{-4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}}{3}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-6{t}^{2}+\\frac{41}{3}t-8 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}t+5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0{t}^{2}+2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 0{t}^{2}+2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(\\frac{5{a}_{1}-8{a}_{2}-18}{3}\\right){t}^{2}+\\left(\\frac{5{b}_{1}-8{b}_{2}+41}{3}\\right)t+\\left(\\frac{5{c}_{1}-8{c}_{2}-24}{3}\\right) \\\\ \\left(\\frac{-4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+9}{3}\\right){t}^{2}+\\left(\\frac{-4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}-13}{3}\\right)t+\\left(\\frac{-4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+15}{3}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coefficients of like powers of $t,$ we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{a}_{1}-8{a}_{2}-18}{3}=0 \\\\ \\frac{-4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+9}{3}=0 \\\\ \\frac{5{b}_{1}-8{b}_{2}+41}{3}=2{a}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{-4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}-13}{3}=2{a}_{2} \\\\ \\frac{5{c}_{1}-8{c}_{2}-24}{3}={b}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{-4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+15}{3}={b}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first and second equations, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=2,\\,{a}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the second and third equations, and solving for ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=-1,\\,{b}_{2}=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these last two values into the fourth and sixth equations, and solving for ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=1,\\,{c}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+3t-2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186564502 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2} & =3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186564504 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y>0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y>0 \\\\ x-y\\ne 1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x-y>0,\\,x-y\\ne 1\\}.\n$$"] [17592186564531 "The least-squares solution $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{b} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ {x}_{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=9.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8(9) & =-9 \\\\ 9{x}_{1} & =-81 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186564532 "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0\\cdot {e}^{2(0)} \\\\ {e}^{2(0)}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-0}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}+1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=3 \\\\ {c}_{2}+1=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we have ${c}_{1}=3.$ From the second equation, we get ${c}_{2}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186564534 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}>0 \\\\ y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-1<x<1 \\\\ y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1<x<1,\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$"] [17592186565420 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-1-t+2{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(t-{t}^{2}) & =3-3t \\\\ -{x}_{1}+(-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})t+(2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =3-3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations, we get ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=-6.$ \n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle [q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3+(-6)=-9.$"] [17592186565421 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 & {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=6 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=6 \\\\ {x}_{2}=4 \\\\ -{x}_{2}=-4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=4.$ From the first equation, we get  ${x}_{1}=6.$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle [A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6+4=10.$"] [17592186565445 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2-t)+{x}_{2}(3-2{t}^{2}) & =3t-4{t}^{2} \\\\ (2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})-{x}_{1}t-2{x}_{2}{t}^{2} & =3t-4{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}=-4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}=-4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ From the  first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle [q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3+2=-1.$"] [17592186565478 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 9 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} & 3{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}6 & 9 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}6 & 9 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}6 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{9}{2} & -3\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+9\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} & =8 \\\\ 6{x}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle [A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}=1.\n$$"] [17592186566217 "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x & \\,16{x}^{2}y+12y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x=0 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}y+12y=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8x({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2)=0 \\\\ 4y(4{x}^{2}+3)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince $4{x}^{2}+3\\ne 0,$ from the second equation of the system, we obtain that\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ 8x({x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ 8x({x}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(0,0).$\n\nNote that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these points, they are also stationary points."] [17592186580228 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $2\\le x\\le 5,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<2 \\\\ {\\int }_{2}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1, & x>5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{x}\\frac{2}{3}(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={-\\frac{1}{3}t(t-8)|}_{2}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}x(x-8)-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+8x-12}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<2 \\\\ \\frac{-{x}^{2}+8x-12}{3}, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1, & x>5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186580240 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge 0,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}2{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & =-{e}^{-2t}{|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}+1 \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ 1-{e}^{-2x}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186580248 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge 1,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}2{e}^{2-2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & =-{e}^{2-2t}{|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{2-2x}+1 \\\\  & =1-{e}^{2(1-x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1, \\\\ 1-{e}^{2(1-x)}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186580268 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge 1,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}{e}^{1-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & =-{e}^{1-t}{|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-x}+1 \\\\  & =1-{e}^{1-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1, \\\\ 1-{e}^{1-x}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186580294 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/4}}{8}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/4}}{8}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/4}}{8}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/4}}{8}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t/4}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x/4}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/4}}{8}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{x/4}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/4}}{8}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-t/4}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x/4}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-t/4}}{8}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/4}}{8}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-x/4}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/4}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/4}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/4}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186580306 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge 3,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<3 \\\\ {\\int }_{3}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{x}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{1-t/3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-t/3}{|}_{3}^{x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-x/3}+1 \\\\  & =1-{e}^{1-x/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<3, \\\\ 1-{e}^{1-x/3}, & x\\ge 3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186580880 "A chi-square random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(k)$ with $k$ degrees of freedom has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ\\left(\\frac{k}{2}\\right)}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(8).$ So, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16Γ(4)}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nRecalling that $Γ(n)=(n-1)!$ for any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(4)=3!=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the probability distribution becomes\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $P(X<3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1}{96}\\cdot 6.30168 \\\\  & ≈0.0656\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186580909 "A chi-square random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(k)$ with $k$ degrees of freedom has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ\\left(\\frac{k}{2}\\right)}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(8).$ So, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16Γ(4)}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nRecalling that $Γ(n)=(n-1)!$ for any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(4)=3!=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the probability distribution becomes\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $P(X<3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186580958 "A chi-square random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(k)$ with $k$ degrees of freedom has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ\\left(\\frac{k}{2}\\right)}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(4).$ So, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4Γ(2)}x{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nRecalling that $Γ(n)=(n-1)!$ for any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(2)=1!=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the probability distribution becomes\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $P(X<9)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<9) & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{9}x{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 3.75560 \\\\  & =0.9389\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186582390 "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/09/09707ef0347a6b67fc39c5247efbe19d22ca69b713738b5e3361cca5f965b021.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}xy{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}xy{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186582391 "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/10/10bcdedc511d069ecf790e06a1fbac49dc9bebd66638363f4e8dad5cbb82c2e9.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nthen we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy)\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and III do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle II covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186582393 "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/e4/e48f172079d6edc0b29356cafa30c82fcae0afd008e469ea8e0ad9a3660d6fd0.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y+y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles II and III do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle I covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186582396 "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/1f/1f03bc85a6cd6f803c87a8fedaf8518af8306a57ff530481810af91a4d93c697.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement III is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186587297 "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}\\}$ and $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}\\}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (or table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(N,Z)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $N$-row and the $Z$-column.\n\n- The pair $(Q,Q)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $Q$-row and the $Q$-column.\n\n- The pair $(Z,Z)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $Z$-row and the $Z$-column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186587320 "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}\\}$ and $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}\\}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times B$ with $x=y-1.$\n\nLet's consider each row in our table.\n\n- If $x=-2,$ then there is no value of $y\\in B$ that satisfies $x=y-1.$\n\n- If $x=-1,$ then the value $y=0$ satisfies $x=y-1.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(-1,0),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $1$st column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=0,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x=y-1.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(0,1),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $3$rd row and the $2$nd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\nAll the other cells in the matrix must be filled with zeros. Therefore, we have the following adjacency matrix:\n\n|  | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |"] [17592186587329 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{2t/3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{2t/3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-2t/3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{2t/3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{2t/3}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x/3}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{2t/3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{2x/3}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-2t/3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-2t/3}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2x/3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-2t/3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-2t/3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-2x/3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-2x/3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{2x/3}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-2x/3}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186587355 "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}\\}$ and $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}\\}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times B$ with $x\\in y.$\n\nLet's consider each row in our table.\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=\\{1,2\\}$ satisfies $x\\in y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,\\{1,2\\}),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=\\{2\\},\\{1,2\\}$ satisfy $x\\in y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,\\{2\\})$ and $(2,\\{1,2\\}),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $2$nd column and in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=3,$ then there is no value of $y\\in B$ that satisfies $x\\in y.$\n\nAll the other cells in the matrix must be filled with zeros. Therefore, we have the following adjacency matrix:\n\n|  | $∅$ | $\\{2\\}$ | $\\{1,2\\}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $3$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |"] [17592186587358 "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}\\}$ and $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}\\}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (or table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,7)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $2$-row and the $7$-column.\n\n- The pair $(6,5)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $6$-row and the $5$-column.\n\n- The pair $(6,3)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $6$-row and the $3$-column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186587359 "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}\\}$ and $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}\\}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (or table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(1,4)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $1$-row and the $4$-column.\n\n- The pair $(4,9)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $4$-row and the $9$-column.\n\n- The pair $(9,4)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $9$-row and the $4$-column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186589031 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{9,10,11,…,28\\},$ and the set $S=\\{9,10,11,…,28\\}$ has $|S|=28-9+1=20$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{20}, & x=9,10,11,…,28 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X>25$ are $26,27,28.$ There are $3$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X>25)=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{20}=\\frac{3}{20}.\n$$"] [17592186589034 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{8,9,10,…,34\\},$ and the set $S=\\{8,9,10,…,34\\}$ has $|S|=34-8+1=27$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{27}, & x=8,9,10,…,34 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $12\\le X\\le 25$ are $12,13,14,…,25.$ There are $25-12+1=14$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(12\\le X\\le 25)=14\\cdot \\frac{1}{27}=\\frac{14}{27}.\n$$"] [17592186589042 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{-10,-9,-8,…,10\\},$ and the set $S=\\{-10,-9,-8,…,10\\}$ has $|S|=10-(-10)+1=21$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{21}, & x=-10,-9,-8,…,10 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $-3<X\\le 6$ are $-2,-1,0,…,6.$ There are $6-(-2)+1=9$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(-3<X\\le 6)=9\\cdot \\frac{1}{21}=\\frac{3}{7}.\n$$"] [17592186589056 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{2,3,4,…,11\\},$ and the set $S=\\{2,3,4,…,11\\}$ has $|S|=11-2+1=10$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}, & x=2,3,4,…,11 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X\\ge 5$ are $5,6,7,…,11.$ There are $11-5+1=7$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 5)=7\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}=\\frac{7}{10}.\n$$"] [17592186589058 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{2,3,5,6,7,8,9,10\\},$ and the set $S=\\{2,3,5,6,7,8,9,10\\}$ has $|S|=8$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}, & x=2,3,5,6,7,8,9,10 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ there are $3$ items $(8,9,10)$ that satisfy $7<X\\le 10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(7<X\\le 10)=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$"] [17592186589076 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{2,3,4,…,13\\},$ and the set $S=\\{2,3,4,…,13\\}$ has $|S|=13-2+1=12$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{12}, & x=2,3,4,…,13 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=6)=f(6)=\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$"] [17592186589077 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{5,6,7,…,37\\},$ and the set $S=\\{5,6,7,…,37\\}$ has $|S|=37-5+1=33$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{33}, & x=5,6,7,…,37 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=10)=f(10)=\\frac{1}{33}.\n$$"] [17592186589081 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{-10,-9,-8,…,5\\},$ and the set $S=\\{-10,-9,-8,…,5\\}$ has $|S|=5-(-10)+1=16$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}, & x=-10,-9,-8,…,5 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=5)=f(5)=\\frac{1}{16}.\n$$"] [17592186589089 "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x)$, the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(x)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\text{ }\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is the probability density function of $X.$ \n\nIn this problem, the break point $X$ is chosen uniformly from $[0,2]$, so the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\le x<2.\n$$\n\nWhen the stick is broken at point $X$, it is divided into two pieces: one of length $X$ and the other of length $2-X.$ The longer of the two pieces, $Y$, is therefore given by\n\n$$\nY=\\text{max}(X,2-X).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we define\n\n$$\nr(x)=\\text{max}(x,2-x).\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the integral, we express $r(x)$ as an explicit piecewise function. Observe that,\n\n- if $0\\le x\\le 1$, then $x\\le 2-x$, so the longer piece has length $2-x$,\n\n- if $1<x\\le 2$, then $x>2-x$, so the longer piece has length $x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2-x, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ x, & 1<x\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[\\text{max}(X,2-X)] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{1}{2}(2-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the expected length of the longer piece is $\\frac{3}{2}.$"] [17592186589099 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{4,5,8,9,10,12,13\\},$ and the set $S=\\{4,5,8,9,10,12,13\\}$ has $|S|=7$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7}, & x=4,5,8,9,10,12,13 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ there are $6$ items $(5,8,9,10,12,13)$ that satisfy $X\\ge 5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 5)=6\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}=\\frac{6}{7}.\n$$"] [17592186589102 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{5,6,7,8,9,10\\},$ and the set $S=\\{5,6,7,8,9,10\\}$ has $|S|=6$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}, & x=5,6,7,8,9,10 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=8)=f(8)=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$"]}
